child care services in havana will soon  become more affordable to many families  across the valley governor steve sisilak  and other state officials expanding the  fund to pay for child care with some 50  million dollars madison kimbrough  joining us live in our newsroom madison  has those details the cost of child care  is always a topic of discussion among  parents and with these added funds it  will help cover child care co-pays for  those using state subsidized child care  programs  here in nevada chelsea slider works for  the children's cabinet a non-profit  organization that works closely with the  las vegas urban league to offer a  variety of child care services for  southern nevada families services like  child care subsidy and child care  resource referrals whereas the  children's cabinet offers emergency and  infant toddler grants family counseling  and parent education and support slider  says with this boost in funds there's a  whole new group of families who will now  be able to receive support we see people  every day come through our doors that  are  barely making it or they're not making  ends meet you know they're barely  getting by so this is going to provide a  huge amount of financial  stress relief for our families so that  they could go to work and  focus on that and know that their  children are in a high quality  learning environment the program  provided child care help for low income  families who make around 36 thousand  dollars per year and now governor  sisalek says with this extra money  relief is now expanded to those in the  middle income range of up to 70 thousand  dollars per year per family  and for families and providers  interested in the program here in  southern nevada you can head to the  website you see on your screen that's  childcarelv.org  according to governor sisil act the hope  is that these funds will last through  20-23 in the newsroom madison kimbrough  live local now
 

